---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import ComponentGrid from "../../components/ComponentGrid.astro";
import Icon from "../../components/Icon.astro";
import { url } from "../../lib/urls";
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Introduction"
  description="A small foundation for content-first interfaces. Start with semantic HTML, add a few classes, and keep control of the result."
  section="Start here"
  source="website/src/pages/docs/index.astro"
>
  <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
    <p>
      Lineframe UI is a framework-agnostic CSS library with optional JavaScript for theme preference
      and article navigation. It gives you readable typography, warm paper and true-black themes,
      quiet frames, and a reusable set of native components.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is not a page builder, an application framework, or a personal-blog theme. Use it for a
      product guide, a project website, a knowledge base, or a small interface that needs clear
      structure.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="lf-cluster">
    <a class="lf-button lf-button--solid" href={url("docs/installation/")}>
      Install Lineframe <Icon name="arrow-right" />
    </a>
    <a class="lf-button" href={url("docs/philosophy/")}>Read the philosophy</a>
  </div>

  <section class="docs-section">
    <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
      <h2 id="a-simple-mental-model">A simple mental model</h2>
    </div>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Choose the right HTML.</strong>
        <p class="docs-inline-note">
          A link navigates. A button acts. A label names its field. Keep the native behavior.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Add only the classes you need.</strong>
        <p class="docs-inline-note">
          Most styles are opt-in, with <code>lf-</code> names and shared design tokens. Use <code
            >lf-site</code
          > for the page foundation.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Enhance deliberately.</strong>
        <p class="docs-inline-note">
          CSS already handles layout, forms, and feedback. Theme persistence and generated article
          navigation are optional, explicit JavaScript behaviors.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section" aria-label="Component guides">
    <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
      <h2 id="what-is-included">What is included</h2>
      <p>Each guide pairs live examples with the markup that renders them.</p>
    </div>
    <ComponentGrid />
  </section>

  <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
    <h2 id="what-you-own">What you still own</h2>
    <p>
      Your content, routing, business logic, validation, and application state remain yours. So do
      meaningful alt text, sensible heading levels, testing custom colors, and checking the
      completed experience with a keyboard. Start with the <a href={url("docs/accessibility/")}
        >accessibility checklist</a
      >.
    </p>
    <h2 id="no-framework-required">No framework required</h2>
    <p>
      The browser assets have no runtime dependencies. You can use them in plain HTML, a
      server-rendered site, or a component framework. The typed module API supports explicit setup
      and cleanup; importing it does not attach browser listeners.
    </p>
    <p>
      This documentation uses Astro, local fonts, and a little website-only JavaScript. None of
      those are required by your site.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
